<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="The Battaglin Oria 7020 from the later 1990s: an Italian aluminium racer with Shimano Ultegra and Campagnolo Atlanta 1996 rims."/><link rel="canonical" href="https://teob.dk/en/bikes/battaglin-oria-7020/"/><link rel="alternate" hreflang="da" href="https://teob.dk/cykler/battaglin-oria-7020/"/><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://teob.dk/en/bikes/battaglin-oria-7020/"/><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://teob.dk/cykler/battaglin-oria-7020/"/><meta property="og:type" content="article"/><meta property="og:title" content="Battaglin Oria 7020 aluminium racer · The Evolution of Bikes"/><meta property="og:description" content="An Italian aluminium racer where vivid 1990s paint meets visible, readable mechanics."/><meta property="og:image" content={heroSocial}/><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"/><title>Battaglin Oria 7020 aluminium racer · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head><body><EnglishHeader current="bikes" danishPath="/cykler/battaglin-oria-7020/"/>
<main class="atala-page">
<header class="quick-hero"><p class="section-label">In the collection · Italy · c. 1996–1998</p><h1>Battaglin<br/><em>Oria 7020</em></h1><p>An Italian aluminium racer from the part of the 1990s when oversized welded tubes, integrated shift levers and exuberant paint all belonged on the same bicycle.</p></header>
<figure class="quick-pass battaglin-hero"><OptimizedImage src={hero} alt="Blue aluminium Battaglin road bicycle seen from the drive side" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high"/><figcaption>The Battaglin seen from the drive side. Its frame labels, Ultegra parts and Atlanta 1996 rims place the build firmly in the 1990s. Photo: The Evolution of Bikes.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="atala-facts"><div><strong>c. 1996–1998</strong><span>Probable period</span></div><div><strong>Oria 7020</strong><span>Double-butted aluminium</span></div><div><strong>Ultegra</strong><span>Shimano gears and brakes</span></div><div><strong>Atlanta 1996</strong><span>Campagnolo rims</span></div></section>
<section class="atala-opening"><p class="section-label">A bicycle that does not hide</p><p>The blue ground colour is only the beginning. Yellow, red and pink fields run across the tubes, while the Battaglin name appears on the top, down and chain stays. Beneath the paint is a decisive change: the familiar Italian racing bicycle had been welded in aluminium.</p></section>
<section class="atala-story"><div><p class="section-label">Marostica, 1981</p><h2>The rider’s name reached the down tube</h2></div><div><p>Giovanni Battaglin won both the Vuelta a España and the Giro d’Italia in 1981. After that season he began making bicycles under his own name in his home town of Marostica in the Veneto.</p><p>The marque first became known for steel frames. Carrera’s results in the 1980s, including Stephen Roche’s remarkable 1987 season, gave the name an international reach. Experiments with lighter materials such as aluminium and carbon followed.</p><p>This bicycle belongs to that later phase. Giovanni’s signature appears on the frame, but the machine is not built as a memorial to the past. Its material and controls were modern choices for their time.</p></div></section>
<section class="atala-details"><figure><OptimizedImage src={tubing} alt="Aluminium 7020 Oria Double Butted label on the Battaglin frame"/><figcaption>The frame label states Oria 7020 aluminium and double-butted tubing. It is the most secure material information on the bicycle.</figcaption></figure><figure><OptimizedImage src={signature} alt="Yellow Giovanni Battaglin signature on the blue frame"/><figcaption>Giovanni Battaglin’s signature connects the bicycle to the rider who founded the marque after his Giro–Vuelta double.</figcaption></figure><figure><OptimizedImage src={badge} alt="Battaglin emblem on the blue head tube"/><figcaption>The restrained head badge sits almost still amid the paint scheme’s many colours.</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="atala-components"><div><p class="section-label">Aluminium in the 1990s</p><h2>Large tubes and visible welds</h2></div><div><p>7020 is a heat-treatable aluminium alloy used for light and stiff road frames. The Oria label describes these tubes as double butted: thicker near the joints and thinner through the middle where loads are lower.</p><p>The tubes are larger than those of a conventional steel frame. Their diameter increases resistance to bending and twisting without requiring the same wall thickness throughout. TIG welds remain visible as part of the construction.</p><p>The fork, by contrast, is metal and conventionally slender. The bicycle therefore sits between two visual languages: the proportions of the old racing machine and the larger profiles of the aluminium years.</p></div></section>
<section class="atala-details"><figure><OptimizedImage src={paint} alt="Battaglin lettering and multicoloured paint on the top tube"/><figcaption>The paint emphasises the tubes’ size and gives the bicycle an unmistakable 1990s character.</figcaption></figure><figure><OptimizedImage src={cockpit} alt="Handlebar and Shimano Ultegra levers on the Battaglin"/><figcaption>Ultegra STI levers combine braking and shifting at the handlebar, while the cables remain exposed at the front.</figcaption></figure><figure><OptimizedImage src={rim} alt="Campagnolo Atlanta 1996 rim and Grand Prix tyre"/><figcaption>Campagnolo Atlanta 1996 is a model name tied to the Olympic host city. It helps with dating but cannot establish the frame’s year by itself.</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="atala-story"><div><p class="section-label">The collection build</p><h2>Italian frame, Japanese group</h2></div><div><p>The shift levers, derailleurs, brakes and crankset carry Shimano’s Ultegra name. This was a familiar combination on good European road bicycles: an Italian frame could readily receive a Japanese group to suit its owner’s budget and use.</p><p>The double chainset and close-ratio road cassette suit fast road riding. Rim brakes, a threaded headset and a conventional quill stem keep the machine mechanically straightforward.</p><p>The wheels use Campagnolo Atlanta 1996 rims, part of Campagnolo’s series named after Olympic host cities. Their silver profiles provide a calm counterpoint to the colour of the frame.</p></div></section>
<section class="atala-detail-pair"><figure><OptimizedImage src={drivetrain} alt="Shimano Ultegra crankset and rear derailleur on the Battaglin"/><figcaption>The Ultegra build can be read directly from the crankset and derailleurs.</figcaption></figure><div><p class="section-label">Dating</p><h2>A probable period, not a firm model year</h2><p>No frame number or contemporary catalogue has yet established the exact model and year. The bicycle is therefore dated cautiously to about 1996–1998 from its frame type, graphics and build.</p><p>Atlanta 1996 refers to the Olympic city and rim model, not a production stamp for the complete bicycle. “Wing” is likewise not used here as a confirmed model name because it does not appear on the surviving bicycle.</p></div></section>
<section class="koga-specs"><div><p class="section-label">The bicycle</p><h2>Brief specification</h2></div><dl><div><dt>Maker</dt><dd>Battaglin</dd></div><div><dt>Designation</dt><dd>Oria 7020</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>c. 1996–1998, probable</dd></div><div><dt>Frame</dt><dd>Oria 7020 aluminium, double butted</dd></div><div><dt>Fork</dt><dd>Metal, threaded headset</dd></div><div><dt>Gears and brakes</dt><dd>Shimano Ultegra</dd></div><div><dt>Wheels</dt><dd>Campagnolo Atlanta 1996 rims</dd></div><div><dt>Status</dt><dd>In the collection</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="atala-closing"><p>The Battaglin shows more than aluminium becoming lighter and stiffer. It also shows an Italian maker making the new material its own: with a rider’s signature, large lettering and paint that gives technology and temperament equal space.</p><div class="bike-bottom-nav"><a href={`${base}/en/bikes/`}>← Back to bicycles</a><a href={`${base}/en/periods/1990-1999/`}>View the period →</a></div></section>
</main><EnglishFooter/><style>.quick-pass.battaglin-hero img{width:min(100%,960px);height:auto;max-height:72vh;aspect-ratio:auto;object-fit:contain}</style></body></html>
